    "created_at": "2021-06-29T13:54:45Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-06-29T13:54:45Z",
    "body": "> Alignment issues like this are usually caused by character-width support by your shell and terminal emulator. Some shells and emulators require you to enable double-width character support.\r\n> \r\n> Alternatively, you can manually add a space to address spacing issues:\r\n> \r\n> ```shell\r\n> # The symbol may not appear in GitHub, but it's here\r\n> starship config nodejs.symbol \" \"\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> If you continue running into issues, you can run the following to revert starship to using the old \"hex\" symbol for Nodejs:\r\n> \r\n> ```shell\r\n> starship config nodejs.symbol \"⬢ \"\r\n> ```\r\n\r\nAfter your inspiration and several attempts, I found that changing the terminal emulator was the only way to fix the problem and keep the terminal display consistent across multiple programs, because before, the terminal rendered fine in VS Code, even though it was also using Terminal.app, but it just happened to be different.\r\nNow I've replaced them all with iTerm.app as the terminal emulator and get a consistent display. The fonts I was using at the beginning were Cascadia Code PL, which is also available at this point.\r\n\r\nFinally, thank you very much for your answers and help!",
